the city of london like the rest of the  nation is in mourning but it could soon  be time for celebration in the uk's  financial heart as chancellor quasiquato  considers lifting an eu era cap on  bankers bonuses it was part of a package  of measures that were designed to clamp  down on risk-taking behavior in the  financial services industry this  risk-taking behavior was what was many  people think caused the crash at 08. and  the rule is a pretty simple one it says  that  no bank may pay an individual member of  staff  more than twice  his or her  annual compensation in a single year  long opposed by british regulators  lifting it will be an attempt to protect  the city's european dominance  post-brexit  in 2019 there were 3 519 british bankers  earning more than a million euros that's  seven times the next largest germany  with 492  and only france italy and spain were  also in three figures at the top banking  is very well paid average monthly pay in  the sector is seven thousand two hundred  and fifty three pounds that's two and a  half times the uk average of two  thousand eight hundred and sixty-four  but financial and professional services  pay around 100 billion pounds a year in  taxes  uncapping bankers bonuses would send a  strong signal that the new government is  serious about a new economic direction  but in the middle of a cost of living  crisis with wages falling behind  inflation and even the governor of the  bank of england calling for pay  restraint it would leave it open to the  charge that is putting the demands of  the already wealthy ahead of ordinary  families the move is consistent with the  new government's promise to prioritize  growth and cut regulation ministers may  argue that if it helps a rising economic  tide lift all boats the odd yacht can be  excused paul kelso sky news in the city  of london
 

